---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Gallery from '../components/Gallery.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import QuoteForm from '../components/QuoteForm.astro';
import { projectsIn } from '../lib/media';
import { serviceSchema, breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/outdoor-living/';
const title = 'Outdoor Living Structures | Cabanas & Outdoor Kitchens';
const description =
  'Outdoor living structures for South Florida homes — pool cabanas, covered patios, outdoor kitchen covers and media walls, designed and built as one finished space.';

const crumbs = [{ label: 'Outdoor Living' }];

const schema = [
  serviceSchema({
    name: 'Outdoor living structure design and construction',
    description,
    path,
    serviceType: 'Outdoor living structure construction',
    offers: [
      'Pool cabana construction',
      'Covered patio construction',
      'Outdoor kitchen covers',
      'Outdoor media walls',
      'Privacy and shade screening',
    ],
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs, path),
  webPageSchema({ path, name: title, description }),
];

const elements = [
  {
    name: 'Pool cabanas',
    body: 'A freestanding structure at the far side of the pool that turns the deck into a destination rather than a walkway. Usually where the kitchen, the bar and the seating all end up.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Covered patios',
    body: 'A solid or insulated roof extending the house outward, giving you a genuinely dry, cooler room that furniture can live in permanently.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Outdoor kitchens',
    body: 'The structure and the kitchen designed together, so headroom, venting, power, water and counter runs all resolve instead of colliding.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Media walls',
    body: 'A purpose-built wall carrying a television and speakers, positioned so the screen is shaded at the hours you actually use it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Privacy screening',
    body: 'Fixed slat panels or roll-down screens on the exposed side — for neighbours, for low western sun, or for both.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Water features',
    body: 'Rain curtains and similar features integrated into the roof edge rather than added beside it. See the cabana project in the gallery.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} crumbs={crumbs} schema={schema}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Outdoor living"
    title="Outdoor Living Structures Built as One Finished Space"
    lede="The best backyards in South Florida are not a pergola plus a grill plus a television. They are one structure that was designed to hold all of it — where the lighting is in the ceiling, the wiring is inside the frame, and the kitchen and the roof were drawn at the same time."
    facts={[
      { label: 'Structures', value: 'Cabanas, covered patios, outdoor kitchen covers, media walls' },
      { label: 'Built in', value: 'Lighting, fans, speakers, screens and water features' },
      { label: 'Works with', value: 'Existing pools, decks and screen enclosures' },
      { label: 'Service area', value: 'Miami-Dade, Broward and the Upper Keys' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>Why designing it as one thing matters</h2>
      <p>
        Most backyards get built in layers, years apart. The pool goes in, then the deck,
        then someone adds a grill, then a shade structure gets put over part of it, and
        finally a television gets mounted somewhere it half works. Each step is reasonable on
        its own, and the result is a space that never quite resolves.
      </p>
      <p>
        Designing the structure and everything it carries at the same time changes the
        outcome in specific, visible ways. Lighting sits recessed in a soffit instead of
        clamped to a beam. Speaker and television wiring runs inside hollow extrusions
        instead of in surface conduit down a post. A ceiling fan hangs from blocking that was
        put there for it, at a height that actually works. Counter runs and roof edges line
        up. The screen is shaded at four in the afternoon because someone checked where the
        sun would be.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of that is expensive to plan. All of it is expensive — or impossible — to
        retrofit.
      </p>

      <h2>What goes into these structures</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="shell element-wrap">
      <ul class="element-grid">
        {
          elements.map((element) => (
            <li class="element">
              <h3>{element.name}</h3>
              <p>{element.body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-sand">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Our work</p>
        <h2>Outdoor living projects</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:var(--space-s)">
          Cabanas, covered patios and outdoor kitchens we have built across South Florida —
          several photographed mid-installation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Gallery projects={projectsIn('outdoor-living')} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell form-band">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Where to start</h2>
        <p>
          The most useful first message is a description of how you want to use the space —
          cooking, dining, lounging, watching something, all of it — plus photographs from a
          couple of angles and rough dimensions.
        </p>
        <p>
          From there we can tell you whether you are looking at a{' '}
          <a href="/pergolas/">pergola</a>, a full cabana, or something in between, and what
          it takes to build it where you want it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <QuoteForm service="Other Outdoor Project" compact />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="One structure, planned properly."
    body="Tell us everything you want the space to do. It is much cheaper to resolve it on paper than on site."
    secondaryLabel="See the full gallery"
    secondaryHref="/gallery/"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .element-wrap { margin-top: var(--space-l); }
  .element-grid {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr));
  }
  .element { padding-top: var(--space-s); border-top: 2px solid var(--line-strong); }
  .element h3 { font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .element p { margin-top: 0.4rem; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 0.95rem; }

  .form-band { display: grid; gap: var(--space-l); }
  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .form-band { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr); gap: var(--space-2xl); align-items: start; }
  }
</style>
